  about: 'Of the ten thousand verses of the Rig Veda, this single line — twenty-four syllables, the tenth verse of a hymn by the seer Vishvamitra — became the most recited sentence in the history of the world. For three thousand years it has been spoken at dawn and dusk, whispered at the start of study, given to the young as their first sacred words. And here is the first honest thing to say about it, the thing the reverence often hides: it is not a mantra about power, or protection, or paradise. It is a prayer for a clearer mind. It asks the radiant source of everything to do one specific thing — to set our thinking in motion, to quicken our understanding. That a civilization chose, as its holiest daily utterance, a request not for wealth or rescue but for illumination of the intellect, tells you something about what it valued most.',
