      commentary: 'The whole mantra turns on one word, and it is not a word about God — it is a word about the mind. Dhī means thought, insight, the intellect, the visioning faculty; dhiyaḥ is its plural, "our thoughts." The verse asks Savitar to pracodayāt them — to impel, quicken, set them moving in the right direction. Read plainly, the most sacred line in the Veda is a request for mental clarity. Not "give me things"; "sharpen my seeing."\n\nSavitar is the key to why. He is not simply the sun as a disc in the sky; his name comes from the root sū, "to impel, to set in motion." He is the sun as the power that starts everything each morning — that wakes the world, drives the winds, urges the living to rise (the same power the Dawn hymn praises). The prayer takes that cosmic impeller and asks it to do to the mind what it does to the world: start it, move it, illumine it. The one force that turns night into day is asked to turn dullness into insight. That is the whole architecture of the verse — an analogy between sunrise and understanding, made into a petition.\n\nNotice, too, what tat does — "that." The mantra does not name the ultimate; it points past every name toward "that longed-for radiance." This is the same reticence you meet across the Vedic and Upanishadic tradition: the highest thing is gestured at, not captured. And bhargaḥ, "radiance," carries the sense of light that burns away — the darkness dispelled is not out in the world but in the mind that cannot yet see. For a person in an age drowning in information and starving for clarity, it would be hard to compose a more exact prayer than one, three thousand years old, that asks only this: light my thinking.\n\nOne honest note, in the spirit of the whole gallery. The Gayatri as most people chant it today opens with "oṁ bhūr bhuvaḥ svaḥ" — Om, and the names of the three worlds. Those words are a later liturgical preface, added for ritual recitation; they are not part of Rig Veda 3.62.10. The verse itself is exactly what is written above — the prayer for a quickened mind, and nothing more. It needed no embellishment then, and needs none now.'
